    <p>Choosing a sight glass looks deceptively simple — it is a window with a metal ring, how hard can it be? But the window is a pressure boundary, the metal sees the process chemistry, the gasket has to survive both, and the connection has to match your pipework. Specify any of these wrong and you either buy the wrong product twice or discover the mistake under pressure. The reliable way to choose a sight glass is to answer six questions in order: pressure, temperature, materials, application, gasket and mounting. Each answer narrows the field, and by the time you have answered all six, one or two models will remain. This guide walks through the six questions with the numbers you need, and ends with a decision table and the questions buyers ask most.</p>

    <h2 id="pressure">Q1: What Pressure Will the Sight Glass See?</h2>
    <p>Start with pressure because it sets the safety envelope. You need three numbers: the maximum working pressure, the test or burst requirement your specification calls for, and whether the line ever sees full vacuum (FV). Vacuum is the one people forget — a sight glass rated only for positive pressure can be pulled inward and fail in a line that is drained or evacuated.</p>
    <p>The product families have very different pressure ceilings. The <a href="/products/threaded-sight-glasses/">SG-300 threaded sight glass</a> is rated up to 100 bar (1450 psi), which makes it the choice for high-pressure small-bore lines. The <a href="/products/tri-clamp-sight-glasses/">SG-100 tri-clamp sight glass</a> handles full vacuum to 230 psi (1.6 MPa) at up to 300°C, which covers most hygienic duties. <a href="/products/flanged-sight-glasses/">Flanged models</a> follow the flange class — ANSI Class 150 or 300, DIN PN10–PN40. One more rule: window diameter is the main enemy of pressure rating. The larger the window, the lower the pressure the same glass thickness can hold. If you need a big window at high pressure, the glass gets thick and expensive — and sometimes two smaller sight glasses beat one large one.</p>

    <h2 id="temperature">Q2: What Operating Temperature?</h2>
    <p>Temperature determines the glass type and the gasket. Start with the maximum continuous temperature, then check the peak and the rate of change. Continuous temperature is straightforward: soda lime glass is typically limited to around 150–200°C, borosilicate to DIN 7080 to roughly 280–300°C, and quartz to 1000°C and beyond.</p>
    <p>The rate of change matters just as much as the absolute number, because glass fails from thermal shock — a sudden temperature difference across the window — more often than from steady heat. A sight glass on a steam line, a vessel cleaned with hot CIP solution, or a reactor that cycles between ambient and process temperature needs a glass with good thermal shock resistance: borosilicate at minimum, quartz when cycling is severe. Temperature also derates pressure. Most manufacturers specify pressure ratings at ambient temperature, with reduced allowable pressure as temperature climbs — always check the derating curve instead of quoting the ambient rating.</p>

    <h2 id="materials">Q3: What Materials — Glass and Metal?</h2>
    <p>Two material decisions: the glass and the body. For the glass, let temperature and chemistry decide: borosilicate is the default industrial choice, quartz above ~300°C, and soda lime only for low-temperature budget duty. A full comparison of temperature, thermal shock, chemical resistance and cost is in our guide to <a href="/blog/sight-glass-glass-types/">sight glass glass types</a>.</p>
    <p>For the body, match the metal to the process and the environment: 304 stainless steel covers most general and food duties; 316L adds corrosion resistance for chlorides, acids and hygienic cleaning chemicals; carbon steel keeps cost down on non-corrosive media such as oils and clean water; Hastelloy and duplex are available for aggressive chemical service. Wetted materials matter for compliance as well as safety — in <a href="/applications/pharmaceutical/">pharmaceutical</a> and <a href="/applications/food-beverage/">food &amp; beverage</a> lines, the body, gasket and glass all need to meet FDA / USP Class VI requirements.</p>

    <h2 id="application">Q4: What Is the Application and Media?</h2>
    <p>The application decides the form factor. Ask what the media is and what you actually need to see. If you need to check level, color, clarity or mixing in a vessel, a static sight glass is enough. If you need to prove that liquid is flowing through a pipe — especially a clear liquid — you need a <a href="/products/sight-flow-indicators/">sight flow indicator</a> with a flapper, rotary, ball or drip tube element that moves with the flow.</p>
    <p>Industry matters too. Pharmaceutical lines want hygienic tri-clamp construction with dead-space-free bores and USP Class VI materials. Food &amp; beverage wants FDA-compliant gaskets and CIP/SIP tolerance. <a href="/applications/chemical-processing/">Chemical processing</a> emphasizes corrosion resistance and pressure class. <a href="/applications/oil-gas/">Oil &amp; gas</a> favors flanged connections and larger windows. Each application page on this site lists the specific requirements for that sector.</p>

    <h2 id="gasket">Q5: Which Gasket or Seal Material?</h2>
    <p>The gasket is the smallest part of the assembly and the most common cause of premature failure, because it is squeezed between the hot process and the glass on one side and the metal body on the other. Select it for temperature plus chemical compatibility. Common options and their typical continuous limits:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>EPDM</strong> — -40 to 150°C; water, steam, CIP chemicals; the hygienic workhorse.</li>
      <li><strong>NBR (nitrile)</strong> — -30 to 100°C; oils, fuels, general industrial media.</li>
      <li><strong>FKM (Viton)</strong> — -20 to 200°C; oils, fuels, many acids; the high-temperature generalist.</li>
      <li><strong>PTFE</strong> — -200 to 260°C; nearly universal chemical resistance, but less resilient, so it needs careful compression control.</li>
      <li><strong>Silicone</strong> — -60 to 200°C; hygienic and food-contact friendly.</li>
      <li><strong>FFKM</strong> — up to ~300°C; the premium choice for the harshest chemical and thermal duty.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In hygienic lines, confirm the gasket is FDA-compliant and that the assembly geometry allows full drainability. When in doubt between two gasket materials, choose the one that survives the media — a gasket failure is a leak path, not a cosmetic issue.</p>

    <h2 id="mounting">Q6: Which Mounting and Connection Type?</h2>
    <p>Finally, the connection — this is what makes the sight glass fit your pipework. <strong>Tri-clamp</strong> (SG-100): sanitary, fast to disassemble, 1/2"–12" / DN15–DN300; the best choice for food, pharma and hygienic CIP lines. <strong>Threaded</strong> (SG-300): male NPT or BSP from 1/4" to 3", compact and high-pressure; the choice for small lines and tight spaces. <strong>Flanged</strong> (SG-400): ANSI Class 150/300 or DIN PN10–PN40, 2"–8"; the mainstream connection for chemical and oil &amp; gas piping. Weld-end and hybrid options exist for special cases. Choose the connection your pipework already uses — retrofitting a different connection type is the most common avoidable cost in sight glass projects.</p>

    <h2 id="decision-table">Sight Glass Decision Table</h2>
    <p>Once the six questions are answered, the recommendation usually lands in one of these rows:</p>
