        <tr><td>Clear-liquid flow verification</td><td><a href="/products/sight-flow-indicators/">SG-200 Flow Indicator</a></td><td>Borosilicate or soda lime</td><td>Tri-clamp / threaded / flanged / weld</td><td>500°F, 300 psi</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High-pressure small-bore line</td><td><a href="/products/threaded-sight-glasses/">SG-300 Threaded</a></td><td>Borosilicate (DIN 7080)</td><td>NPT / BSP 1/4"–3"</td><td>100 bar / 1450 psi</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Large chemical or oil &amp; gas line</td><td><a href="/products/flanged-sight-glasses/">SG-400 Flanged</a></td><td>Borosilicate / quartz</td><td>ANSI 150/300, DIN PN10–40, 2"–8"</td><td>Per flange class</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High-temperature furnace or reactor</td><td>SG-400 Flanged (quartz option)</td><td>Quartz</td><td>Flanged</td><td>Up to ~1000°C</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>One rule beats all six questions: when in doubt, ask. Send your pressure, temperature, media, gasket preference and connection size to our engineers — you will get a model recommendation and a quotation within 24 hours.</p>

    <h2 id="faq">Sight Glass Selection FAQ</h2>
    <details><summary>What size sight glass do I need?</summary><p>Start from the connection: match the pipe or vessel connection size (tri-clamp, NPT, BSP or flange). For vessels, also consider what you need to see — level range, mixing action or cleaning coverage — and position the window at that height. Larger windows cost more and rate lower in pressure, so spec the smallest window that gives a clear view.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can a sight glass handle full vacuum?</summary><p>Yes, but only if it is specified and rated for vacuum. Full vacuum (FV) is a separate rating from positive pressure — the SG-100 tri-clamp series is rated FV to 230 psi, and other models are rated per design. State explicitly in your inquiry that the line sees vacuum.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Borosilicate or soda lime for my application?</summary><p>Borosilicate unless your duty is clearly low-temperature (below ~150–200°C), non-aggressive and budget-sensitive. Borosilicate costs more but survives steam, CIP and thermal cycling that will eventually crack soda lime. The price difference is small compared with the cost of replacing a failed window on a live line.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which gasket should I use for steam service?</summary><p>EPDM handles low-pressure steam to ~150°C and is the standard hygienic choice. For higher temperatures, step up to FKM or FFKM — but remember the glass and body must also tolerate the steam temperature, and the glass type usually becomes the limiting factor above 200°C.</p></details>
